# Database Security & PII Encryption

## 🔐 Core Security Rules

### PII Fields MUST Be Encrypted
All Personally Identifiable Information encrypted with pgcrypto:
- **Auth**: `google_connector_ref`, `github_connector_ref`, `hashed_password`, `encrypted_private_key`
- **Profiles**: `first_name`, `last_name`, `email`, `phone_number`, `address`, `bio`, `profile_picture_url`, `academic_institution`, `academic_email`

### Encryption Key Management
- ✅ Use `sqlc.arg(encryption_key)` parameter pattern
- ✅ Keys from environment variables only
- ❌ NEVER hardcode encryption keys

## ⚡ Critical Type Safety

### ALWAYS Cast CASE Expressions
sqlc loses type information with CASE expressions - explicit casting required:

- **Nullable columns** → `::text`
- **Non-nullable columns** → `::varchar`

❌ **Never**:
```sql
CASE WHEN condition THEN value ELSE NULL END  -- Generates interface{}
```

✅ **Always**:
```sql
CASE WHEN condition THEN value ELSE NULL END::text     -- Generates string
CASE WHEN condition THEN value ELSE NULL END::varchar  -- Generates string
```

## 🔄 Encryption Patterns

### INSERT/UPDATE (Encrypt)
```sql
first_name = CASE 
    WHEN sqlc.narg(first_name) IS NOT NULL 
    THEN pgp_sym_encrypt(sqlc.narg(first_name), sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
    ELSE first_name
END::text
```

### SELECT (Decrypt)
```sql
CASE 
    WHEN first_name IS NOT NULL 
    THEN pgp_sym_decrypt(first_name::bytea, sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
    ELSE NULL 
END::text as first_name
```

## 📝 Complete Example
```sql
-- name: UpdateProfile :one
UPDATE profiles SET 
    first_name = CASE 
        WHEN sqlc.narg(first_name) IS NOT NULL 
        THEN pgp_sym_encrypt(sqlc.narg(first_name), sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
        ELSE first_name
    END::text,
    email = CASE 
        WHEN sqlc.narg(email) IS NOT NULL 
        THEN pgp_sym_encrypt(sqlc.narg(email), sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
        ELSE email
    END::text
WHERE id = sqlc.arg(id)
RETURNING 
    id,
    CASE 
        WHEN first_name IS NOT NULL 
        THEN pgp_sym_decrypt(first_name::bytea, sqlc.arg(encryption_key))
        ELSE NULL 
    END::text as first_name;
```

## 🎯 Generated Go Types

With correct patterns, sqlc generates optimal types:

**Input Parameters:**
```go
FirstName pgtype.Text `json:"first_name"`  // Nullable
PublicKey string      `json:"public_key"`  // Required
```

**Return Values:**
```go
FirstName string `json:"first_name"`      // Decrypted PII
```

## ✅ Development Checklist

Before committing database changes:
- [ ] PII fields identified and encrypted
- [ ] Encryption key parameter: `sqlc.arg(encryption_key)`
- [ ] Type casting applied: `::text` or `::varchar`
- [ ] Test generation: `bun db:generate`
- [ ] Verify no `interface{}` types in generated Go code

## 🚨 Security Red Flags
- PII stored unencrypted
- Hardcoded encryption keys
- Missing type casting (generates `interface{}`)
- SQL injection vulnerabilities

## 🛠️ Development Commands
```bash
bun db:generate     # SQL → Go code generation
bun db:migrate:up   # Run migrations
bun compose:up      # Start PostgreSQL
```

## Database Structure
**Core Tables**: `authentication_credentials`, `profiles`, `events`, `event_attendees`, `event_certificates`
**Extensions**: uuid-ossp, pg_trgm, citext, pgcrypto

**Environment Variables**:
```env
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
PII_ENCRYPTION_KEY=your-key-from-env
```